{"qid":"Q911440","names":["Protectorate of the Western Regions"],"title":"Protectorate of the Western Regions","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Protectorate of the Western Regions (西域都護府) was the Han dynasty's principal administrative instrument for governing the Tarim Basin and adjacent steppe zones west of the Yumen Pass, established in 59 BC when the Han court appointed Zheng Ji as the first Protector-General. At its operational peak it oversaw tribute relations with thirty-six or more city-states — including Loulan, Khotan, and Kashgar — controlling the Silk Road oasis corridors against Xiongnu interference. The protectorate's authority collapsed repeatedly during the Eastern Han period and was defunct by around AD 107 under renewed nomadic pressure, revived intermittently, and our dataset attests it through 601, reflecting its partial successor functions under subsequent Chinese dynasties in the region. Chinese control effectively lapsed with the fall of the Han in 220 and was not consolidated again until the Tang Anxi Protectorate in the 7th century.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The protectorate's nominal jurisdiction over named city-states is documented in Han shu administrative registers; actual territorial control was indirect and episodic, making spatial extent inherently approximate and the dataset's extended to-year of 601 a maximum-interpretation boundary.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Protectorate of the Western Regions","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protectorate_of_the_Western_Regions"},{"label":"Britannica: Silk Road","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Silk-